<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bida Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1856 CE – 1901 CE (approx.) · capital Bida</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Fulani emirate that ruled the Nupe from Bida, a centre of crafts and slave raiding on the middle Niger until the Royal Niger Company's victory in 1897.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallam-dendo">Mallam Dendo</a> gained influence in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nupe-kingdom">Nupe Kingdom</a> by exploiting civil wars among its princes, and his sons took control of the country. After further conflicts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-zaki">Usman Zaki</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaba">Masaba</a> emerged as rulers, and around 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bida">Bida</a> was founded as the capital of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> owing allegiance to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu Emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaba">Masaba</a>, who ruled from 1859 to 1873, built a strong state with a large army and a network of fiefs held by royal princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> raided its neighbours heavily for slaves, who worked on royal estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bida">Bida</a>. The city was famous for its glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, brass and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> work and woven cloth, and it traded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> with European merchants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In January 1897 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abubakar's army outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bida">Bida</a>. British forces occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in 1901 within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etsu-nupe">Etsu Nupe</a> still reigns at Bida.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bida-emirate.jpg" alt="Bida Emirate" width="320" height="306"><figcaption>Image: JelaniZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Place_of_Etsue_Nupe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1856 CE – 1901 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bida</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Usman Zaki, Masaba, Umaru Majigi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nupe, Fulfulde, Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nupe Emirate, Emirate of Bida</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1878&amp;focus=bida-emirate" aria-label="Bida Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 1838 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bida Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 2094L5166 2094M5166 2094L5128 1967M5166 2094L5187 2202M5166 2094L5170 2068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5166 2094h0M5128 1967h0M5187 2202h0M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 2094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 2094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bida. <a href="/atlas?year=1878&amp;focus=bida-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
